course: dictionary
lesson: 15961
title: Untraceable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Untraceable means impossible to find, follow or trace. If nobody can follow the path back to something, it is untraceable. «The phone call was untraceable, so the police could not find out who made it.» The clue is that the police could not find out who made the call, so untraceable must mean impossible to find.
explain: Look at the word parts. The root word is trace, which means to find or follow a path. Un- means not, and -able means can be done. So untraceable means cannot be traced. «The thief left no fingerprints, and the stolen money was untraceable.»
explain: Scientists use the same word for things that are too small to find. «The chemical was in amounts so tiny that it was untraceable.» Here it does not mean hidden away. It means so small that nobody can detect or measure it.
fact: Untraceable means impossible to find, follow or trace.
fact: The root word trace means to find or follow a path.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so untraceable means cannot be traced.
fact: «Untraceable» can describe a phone call, a person, a payment or a poison.
fact: An untraceable amount is so small that it cannot be found or measured.
exercise: «The phone call was untraceable, so the police could not find who made it.» What does untraceable mean here? | Very loud and clear | Easy to trace back to the caller | *Impossible to find or follow | Sent by post
exercise: Find and copy the word that tells you the root of untraceable. | untraceable is the root word | un | *trace | able
exercise: «The chemical was in amounts so tiny that it was untraceable.» What does untraceable mean here? | Sweet tasting | Very large and heavy | Bright and glowing | *So small it cannot be found or measured
test: What is the opposite of untraceable? | *Easy to trace | Hidden | Impossible to see | Unknown
test: «The message was sent from an account that was untraceable.» What does this tell you about the account? | It was very loud | It was sent by post | *Nobody could find out who was behind it | Everyone knew the owner's name
exam: «The poison was untraceable, so the police could not prove who had used it.» What does untraceable suggest? | Very bright in colour | Known to everyone in the town | Easy to test in a laboratory | *Impossible to find or follow back to its source
